mod std.io.coroutines;

import ( std.io )

/**
 * holding_dispatcher - what a dispatcher ALSO is when it KEEPS the coroutines
 * it is given instead of letting them start: they start when it says so. A
 * class implements both: `class my_dispatcher : coroutine_dispatcher, holding_dispatcher`.
 *
 * It is what a test dispatcher is (std.guikit.testing: test_dispatcher()):
 * the work a model starts must not run until the test asks for it, and then
 * in the order it was started. An ordinary dispatcher only picks a thread; it
 * cannot stop a coroutine from starting - on a thread that is busy too, the
 * runtime gives a waiting fiber its turn sooner or later.
 *
 * start_coroutine (builders.sharp) hands every coroutine placed on a
 * holding_dispatcher to hold() and does not start it. Whoever holds it calls
 * start_held(coroutine) later; select_thread() then says where it runs, as
 * for any dispatcher. A coroutine started INSIDE held work, without a
 * dispatcher of its own, takes its parent's - so it is held too.
 *
 * Kotlin equivalent: StandardTestDispatcher, whose scheduler keeps the tasks
 * until advanceUntilIdle() / runCurrent().
 */
interface holding_dispatcher {
    /** keep this coroutine: it is attached to its parent, and has not started */
    def hold(coroutine: job);
}

/** start a coroutine a holding_dispatcher was given to keep */
def start_held(coroutine: job) {
    coroutine.start();
}
